          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had reflux for years and now food sometimes sticks going down. How worrying is that, and what does guidance describe about when to investigate?",
          "answer": "Please book a review with your doctor soon, and do not wait for your next routine appointment. Food sticking going down, in someone with years of reflux, is something doctors want to examine rather than assume is simple reflux. An automated check of your description found no emergency pattern, but that is not a clearance. The check only covers a fixed list of presentations.\n\nThe GERD guidance (*GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it*) lists difficulty swallowing, or pain on swallowing, as an \"alarm feature.\" When it is present, the guidance recommends prompt endoscopy (a camera test of the gullet and stomach) rather than simply trying more reflux treatment. The same guidance lists other alarm features: unintentional weight loss, vomiting blood or black tarry stools, iron-deficiency anaemia, persistent vomiting, a lump felt in the abdomen, a family history of upper gastrointestinal cancer, and new symptoms at an older age. The *Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)* summary says long-standing acid exposure can cause inflammation, narrowing (stricture) that makes food stick, and Barrett's oesophagus, a change in the lining that carries a small increased risk of cancer. Only a clinical assessment, usually including endoscopy, can tell these apart.\n\nSeek urgent care now if you are vomiting blood, passing black tarry stools, cannot keep fluids down, are struggling to swallow your own saliva, or are choking. Also seek urgent care for drooling, trouble breathing, or chest pain or pressure, especially with sweating, breathlessness, or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or neck. Do not assume chest pain is reflux. These emergency points are general safety advice, not taken from a dedicated emergency source.\n\nThe sources do not say how long symptoms must last before action, or whether difficulty with solids versus liquids changes urgency. They also do not grade how strong the evidence is for swallowing difficulty predicting cancer or stricture. I found no disagreement between guidance documents on when to scope for swallowing difficulty; the disagreements I found concerned thyroid and diabetes guidance, which do not apply here. Your doctor can judge how urgent your situation is and whether it needs endoscopy or other testing.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
